prompt:
Use Three.js and TransformControls to implement a scene in which selectable objects can be translated, rotated, and scaled.

[Three.js Import Method (Must Be Followed Strictly)]
Use the following importmap in <head>, and import through ES Modules in <script type="module">:
<script type="importmap">
{ "imports": {
    "three": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/build/three.module.js",
    "three/addons/": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/examples/jsm/"
} }
</script>
<script type="module">
import * as THREE from 'three';
import { OrbitControls } from 'three/addons/controls/OrbitControls.js';
import { TransformControls } from 'three/addons/controls/TransformControls.js';
// Write your code here
</script>

[Objects]
(1) Exactly 5 objects: box, sphere, cylinder, torus, and cone; their names must match __3D_STATE__.objects[].name;
(2) Click an object to select it and attach TransformControls; clicking empty space must either deselect the object or retain the last selected object (the behavior must be fixed and documented in a comment);

[Mode Buttons]
(3) id="translateBtn" → translate; (4) id="rotateBtn" → rotate; (5) id="scaleBtn" → scale;

[Snapping]
(6) id="snapToggle": when enabled, translation snaps to a 0.5 grid and rotation snaps to 15° (π/12); when disabled, transformations are continuous;

[Information Panel]
(7) Display the currently selected object's position, rotation (using the same unit as __3D_STATE__: degrees or radians must be consistent), and scale;

[Undo and Redo]
(8) id="undoBtn" and id="redoBtn": undo and redo transformations step by step (position/rotation/scale history stacks);

[State Exposure (Required)]
(9) Update window.__3D_STATE__ every frame:
  - objectCount (always 5)
  - selectedObject: string|null
  - mode: 'translate'|'rotate'|'scale'
  - snapEnabled: boolean
  - objects:[{name,x,y,z,rx,ry,rz,sx,sy,sz}] (consistent with the scene)

[Evaluation Helpers (Recommended)]
(10) window.__STATEPROBE_select__(name), window.__STATEPROBE_nudge__(dx,dy,dz).

Output a single HTML file that runs when opened directly in a browser.
